is the pandemic good or bad for the  spirits industry liquor stores were  considered an essential business at the  start of the lockdown but at the same  time we saw restaurants and hotels were  shut down decimating sales of alcohol  almost two years into covent now a trend  is emerging this holiday season in which  more americans are opting for no or low  alcoholic beverages  so here now to tell us what he sees in  global spirit consumption is bacardi  executive vice president and chief  financial officer tony latham tony  welcome to quick take  thanks colin how are you good good i  want to go back to my first rhetorical  question to you has the pandemic been  good or bad for your industry  as you mentioned uh you know previous  prior to the pandemic we we represent  about 30 of our revenue from the  on-premise from bars restaurants night  clubs sporting venues uh obviously as  you mentioned that that industry was  decimated by covid so i would say by and  large the pandemic has not been good for  the industry the hospitality industry in  general we did see people switch their  consumption moments from you know from  those on-premise outlets to increasing  their consumption on home but i  certainly wouldn't say it's been good  for us or our industry  got it okay so on the whole it has not  been good but people are changing the  way that they consume their drinks so  let's fast forward to december 2021  because it's the season for a holiday  party so i'm a little surprised to hear  of this trend of no or low alcoholic  beverages taking hold fill us in on how  that's changing the way once again  people are  consuming their drinks  well health and wellness has been a  major trend that's been evolving for a  number of years now it's impacted many  many industries and the alcoholic  beverage industry is no different  people are looking to consume in  moderation we're seeing people consume  better quality more super premium  categories and brands as well but we're  responding to that we're responding to  that with our innovation we're launching  uh multiple uh low alcohol and no  alcohol brands into the marketplace in  fact gregory's essences which i've got  just here is a lower alcohol content  brand and we've launched that in the  last nine to 12 months it's been one of  our most successful innovations so the  key for us is to respond to what the  consumers are asking for  got it got it so how how does that break  down for your sales you mentioned that  people are perhaps opting for more  premium products what does that mean for  your lineup  well we're very happy with the portfolio  that we've got our portfolio skews  towards super premium brands like grey  goose tequila patron angels envy  american whiskey  do say cognac etc so we feel very well  positioned with the trend to super  premium consumption at the moment  what kind of drinks are out of favor as  a result or is there just less  consumption of that as people switch  over for now  as opposed to a permanent shift  i wouldn't call that any one category as  being out of favor at the moment um  again what we're seeing we're seeing  trends towards super premium premium  vodka seems to be back which is great  for our great goose brand  tequila is a very hot category at the  moment cognac and uh all whiskeys are in  hot demand at the moment we've also  introduced a range of ready to drink or  premium prepared cocktails as we prefer  to call them  again which provides the convenience and  portability for consumers across  categories so we're seeing great growth  in our premium prepared cocktails i've  got some bacardi and bombay sapphire  examples here in front of me yeah  meeting consumers where they are right  now  you know as we talk about this trend  towards wellness and people perhaps  opting for low or no alcoholic beverages  we've also seen dry january become a  real thing over the last couple of years  i don't remember it being  something that people talked about  before 2012 or so but really now this is  this idea of abstaining from excesses uh  following all the end of the year uh  celebrations  why do you think this is what do you  think  is so compelling about this idea now  as you mentioned it's been around for  nearly a decade now but certainly  gaining in popularity i think that that  mega trend of uh health and wellness and  people being  more aware of uh of embracing that trend  has certainly been an impact there but  again we're we're looking to um to  capitalize on that by having offerings  that allow people to either enjoy in  moderation during that period or abstain  in in total with some of our no and low  alcohol offerings so we don't think  that's going to be a you know a major  new development this particular year um  distinct from from the recent previous  years yeah just building on this  emerging trend overall all right well  one thing that has become a big issue  over the last two years especially since  the start of covid is supply chain  challenges where do you see the  shortages as most acute  yeah there's a couple of elements to the  supply chain challenges first of all you  know actually getting supply of of  products i have to say that uh our  global supply chain group  has done a phenomenal job in keeping  most of our products in supply  throughout the pandemic uh you know  long-term strategic relationships with  suppliers keeping our factories up and  running etc so that's been a that's been  a good thing for us the other thing of  course that we're seeing is inflation  starting to come through  so we talked a little bit earlier about  how prices are rising for all kinds of  food products and drink products and  bacardi is no exception as well talk a  little bit about the price increases  that you've instituted  over the past year and  the range of increases you've put in  yeah sure so as i mentioned earlier it's  key that you look at pricing through a  strategic lens and  different brands different categories  different markets are impacted very  differently by the inflationary  pressures and so we look at we look at  that through a quite detailed  sophisticated lens to make sure that  we're taking pricing  where our cost base is impacted the most  and where we feel our brands have the  the equity to accommodate that i would  say you know on average we've taken  pricing of around three percent over the  last 12 months and we're constantly  reviewing that again it's very important  to look at that at a category and market  and price tier level  most of the inflation we've seen to date  has largely been on logistics and  distribution we're starting to see  inflation come through on raw materials  and packaging and on labor so we expect  greater inflationary impacts as we head  into our next calendar year and how long  do you wait before you decide to start  raising prices uh because of raw  material costs and because of labor  costs  again it depends on the market depends  on the competitive context the retailer  environment but again we're monitoring  this very very closely and uh where you  know our teams are on the ball and we're  taking pricing when we feel it's um both  necessary and uh and the consumers are  prepared to accept it  got it got it before we go um i do want  to ask you because you're at your drink  station right now what is your number  one recommended cocktail as we get ready  to celebrate the start of another new  year  well it's a great question there's uh  there's a lot of kind of festive  partying going on at the moment um we  feel the gray gut the grey goose martini  is back it's uh it's having a  renaissance moment at the moment um so  martinis made with grey goose are really  flying at the moment and then uh the  angels envy old fashion is another  popular uh drink particularly in the  colder climates so  we're seeing lots of uptake on both  those at the moment great goose martini  and an old fashioned love it thank you  so much for cardi cfo tony latham  joining us here
 

